            <ArticleTitle>The effects of race/ethnicity and income on early childhood asthma prevalence and health care use.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>428-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Asthma is the most common chronic illness among US children and is most prevalent in low-income and minority groups. We used multivariate models to disentangle the effects of race/ethnicity, income, and other individual-level risk factors on asthma in a population-based sample of children aged 3 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data are from the 1988 National Maternal and Infant Health Survey and 1991 Longitudinal Follow-Up. Odds ratios of asthma prevalence, hospitalization, and emergency room use were estimated, with control for socioeconomic characteristics, health behaviors, and insurance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Asthma prevalence, hospitalization, and emergency room use declined with increasing income for non-Black but not Black children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lifetime income and sociodemographic characteristics do not explain the excess risks of asthma and emergency health care use for asthma among young Black children.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
